infographic sequence-ascending-steps
data
  title The NAT-traversal escalation ladder
  desc ICE climbs from the cheapest direct path to a guaranteed relay — each rung trades latency for a higher chance of connecting
  order asc
  sequences
    - label STUN — discover the public mapping, connect directly
      desc A STUN server tells each peer the public IP and port the NAT assigned it. Peers then try a direct path. No relay, lowest latency — this is the path you want.
    - label ICE — iterate every candidate until one path works
      desc The framework races STUN-discovered, direct, and relayed candidates together, picking the first working pair. It is the logic that decides whether STUN was enough or TURN is needed.
    - label TURN — relay all traffic through the server
      desc If direct fails (symmetric NAT, CGNAT), a TURN server forwards every packet. It always works but adds a latency penalty and server cost — the fallback of last resort.
theme
  palette #10b981 #f59e0b #f43f5e
